//! `cargo xtask wa-run <command>`: the real run of the WhatsApp backend, step by step, on a profile
//! that keeps its login (T1; the runbook is `docs/whatsapp-run.md`, which comes with the steps).
//!
//! It is `smoke`'s tool for WhatsApp, written beside it and not on top of it: the dozen
//! network-neutral helpers it needs are COPIED below (each names the line of `smoke.rs` it comes
//! from), so `smoke.rs` stays untouched and a change to one tool cannot move the other.
//!
//! ```text
//! cargo xtask wa-run start --confirmed-by-user [--profile <name>]   # the preflight, then the app
//! cargo xtask wa-run tail                                           # HE types the digits (no echo)
//! cargo xtask wa-run answer                                         # HE types y or n to a step's question
//! cargo xtask wa-run step <name>        # one named step: what to do on the phone, the proof
//! cargo xtask wa-run state | report | stop | backup | wipe | rehearse
//! ```
//!
//! # The rules this tool keeps
//!
//! * A real run is never a test. `start` needs the user's go in that session
//!   (`--confirmed-by-user`) and refuses to run under the test lock or inside a test; only the
//!   rehearsal (`--rehearsal-place`, a throwaway place below `target/wa-run-rehearsal/`, the fake
//!   playing WhatsApp, no network) is exempt, and it never touches a kept profile.
//! * A kept profile lives only in `${XDG_DATA_HOME:-~/.local/share}/kourier-wa-run/<name>` (mode
//!   0700), outside the repository, its worktrees and its build directories, and apart from the
//!   app's own data directory and Signal's smoke profiles. No test, demo, perf run, clean-up or
//!   agent reads, writes or deletes anything there; only this tool does.
//! * The last digits of the test account's number are never an argument, an environment variable
//!   of a process an agent can read, a file or a line of output: HE types them into `wa-run
//!   tail` (the prompt reads the terminal with echo off). So the parser REFUSES an
//!   `--expect-tail` and never echoes what follows it.
//! * What the tool prints or writes is meant for someone who must not see names, numbers or
//!   texts: counts, kinds, states and times only.
//! * The app is the debug build of main's HEAD with a clean tree, run from a stamped copy that is
//!   execute-only (mode 0100: nobody can read its environment, where the digits are, from its first
//!   instruction on) and started directly by the supervisor, never through a wrapper; a migration
//!   that ran on a profile is frozen: a change is a new number.
//!
//! # The steps and the send rules (T2)
//!
//! The steps are R1's (`link`, `history`, `history-interrupt`, `passive`, `reconnect`, `receive`,
//! `group-receive`, `read`), the read-only ones (`settings`, `groups`, `call`, `archive-message`,
//! `call-then-archive`, `call-into-archived`: each asserts that the frame count did not move), the
//! peer's acts (`peer-react`, `peer-edit`, `peer-delete`: he acts on the personal phone, nothing
//! is sent) and R2's (`send`, `group-send`, `react`, `edit`, `delete`, `resend`). A question to
//! the user (`call`, `groups`, the archive cases, the resend) is answered by HIM in `wa-run
//! answer`: one key read from his terminal, never an argument, flag or variable.
//!
//! A frame (a `send`, or an `act` that reacts, edits or deletes) reaches the app only when ALL of
//! this holds, each part checked by the supervisor, none by a step:
//!
//! * the run was started with `--for-send` (a linked profile, never with `--for-link` or
//!   `--call-builtin`); a run without it forwards no send and no act;
//! * the text is one of the tool's own constants named by an id (`SEND_TEXTS`, `EDIT_TEXT`,
//!   `REACT_EMOJI`): no text, emoji or reply of a caller's ever reaches the app;
//! * the open chat is the one `receive` (the 1:1 chat) or `group-receive` (the group) remembered,
//!   and the peer wrote into it in THIS start, which the supervisor itself saw (`peer_mark`,
//!   `peer_seen`);
//! * an act takes only a message the tool sent in this start (`Shared::own`);
//! * the chat (and, for an act, the message) rides in the command itself (`conversation`,
//!   `message`, C7): the app checks them on its own thread in the call that sends or acts, so a
//!   click into another chat between the supervisor's look and the frame is refused with nothing
//!   sent; a frame without them is refused in `forward_to_app`, the one place a command leaves;
//! * EVERY frame counts before it leaves (10 per start, 30 per profile; `--reset-frame-budget` is
//!   his word) and the tool counts THE WIRE: the backend says each frame of Kourier's send path in
//!   two count lines at its one write point (`a send frame was written`, `a send frame was
//!   answered`: [`FRAME_WRITTEN_LINE`], [`FRAME_ANSWERED_LINE`]), and the supervisor counts them.
//!   ANY nack halts the run and stops the app: a refused or failed frame, an `answered` line with
//!   any outcome but `acked` (a 429, a 5xx), no `acked` line within [`ACK_BOUND`] of a frame the
//!   tool forwarded (the supervisor's own clock), and a `written` line beyond what the tool
//!   forwarded and the start owes (an unforwarded frame). A halt cannot cancel the core's queued
//!   retry, only stop the app before it runs: the state file keeps the frames whose `acked` line was
//!   not seen (`unacked`, written before the frame leaves), and the next start OWES them (counted
//!   into its ten and the profile's thirty before the app starts, a `--for-send` start only). Which
//!   answers leave a frame owed (T2 G1): the `answered` line's `again=no` is a final answer for the
//!   core (a refusal, a permanent failure), so the frame is SETTLED (`unacked` minus 1; it stays
//!   counted), while `again=<seconds>` keeps it owed and records `owed_not_before`, which refuses a
//!   send start inside the wait; no `again=` is `again=0` (owed); the app's own guard refusing a
//!   frame (`guardRefused`) settles it, any other refusal or timeout leaves it owed. A frame the
//!   backend wrote that the tool did not forward is COUNTED before the run halts on it (G2).
//!   `--reset-frame-budget` keeps the owed frames (G3); `--forget-owed-frames` is the user's word that they will not come (a
//!   final failure before the wire, a retry past the message's 24 h budget): the owed count and its date go, the old values
//!   stay in the state file as `owed_forgotten`, and no frame is given back (H1). The
//!   frame the tool does not forward is the app's own same-id resend after a kill, which the
//!   `resend` step arms with his y: its `unacked` is such an owed frame. The resend's record in the
//!   state file (`resend`) is written under the frame gate: `armed` (his y, the reserved frame, the
//!   number of THE START: an `armed` record of an earlier start is dropped when a start begins),
//!   `sending` before the text leaves, `sent` with its message, `unknown` after a halt that came
//!   before the message was known; any open record refuses a second resend and a start without
//!   `--for-send` until `step resend`'s call 2 closes it on his look at the personal phone (F2, F4);
//! * a start with a resend record that has no message (`sending`, `unknown`) adopts no message:
//!   call 2 sends nothing and judges the owed frame by the backend's own lines.
//!
//! `--call-builtin` is one start with upstream's call handler (debug build only, the variable set
//! for that start's app and proved by the app's own warning line; his D9).
//!
//! # The preflight
//!
//! [`preflight`] is a list of checks, each its own function that takes what it reads as an
//! argument (the environment, the repository root, a [`Git`] runner, the cgroup text, a clock),
//! so a test can make each refusal on its own and assert ITS text, whatever test run it is
//! itself part of.

